  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...The aforementioned trough\nwill swing through the forecast area on Monday, with gusty \nwinds and periods of showers continuing throughout the day. Snow\nlevels will gradually lower as the trough passes, down to \n5500-6500 ft by Monday afternoon. Showers will taper off Monday \nnight as the trough exits toward the northeast. Beginning early\nTuesday, there is general guidance agreement on a plume of \nmoisture steering into our area from the south by a deep upper \nlow digging into the California coast. The exact magnitude and \nextent of precipitation remains slightly uncertain. For now, the\narea will see the return of moderate to high precipitation \nchances through most of Tuesday. Troughing from this upper low \nwill bring a slight cooling trend on Tuesday, dropping snow \nlevels into the 5000-6500 ft MSL range from north to south. As \nsuch, the central ID mountains will see better chances of light \nsnow accumulations between 2 to 4 inches Tuesday morning through\nearly afternoon. Precipitation will taper off to the east come \nTuesday evening as the upper low steers further east across the\nGreat Basin. Wednesday through early Thursday, a positively \ntilted ridge will build in behind the upper low, keeping our \narea dry. Temperatures are looking to hover near seasonal \nnormals Wednesday through Thursday, with snow levels staying in \nthe 4000-5000 ft MSL range. A relatively weak upper trough will \ndig into the Pacific northwest region Thursday through Friday, \nwith considerable uncertainty on precipitation timing and \nextend. For now, Thursday through early Friday will see the \nreturn of low to moderate valley rain and mountain snow chances \nareawide. Temperatures will stay near or slightly below normal \nthrough Friday.",
